<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord of Sipán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 250 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Señor de Sipán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lord of Sipán is the name given to a Moche ruler whose undisturbed tomb was discovered in 1987 at Huaca Rajada near Sipán on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by the archaeologist Walter Alva. Buried around 250 CE with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> regalia and sacrificed attendants, he is thought to have been a warrior-priest. The find transformed understanding of Moche society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan.jpg" alt="Lord of Sipán" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cicero Moraes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Se%C3%B1or_de_S%C3%ADpan_-_Reconstrucci%C3%B3n_Facial_Forense.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
